<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Belém</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1616">1616</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Belém do Pará</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Belém is a port city near the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amazon">Amazon</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> and the capital of the state of Pará. The Portuguese founded it in 1616 as the fort of Presépio to secure the river against French, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> and English rivals. It became the capital of the State of Grão-Pará and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maranhao">Maranhão</a> in 1751, a centre of the Cabanagem revolt of 1835, and grew rich during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rubber">rubber</a> boom of the late nineteenth century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/belem.jpg" alt="Belém" width="320" height="114" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Burn86 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:L1010748-L1010765_copie_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Belém lies about 100 km upriver from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-ocean">Atlantic Ocean</a>, on the Pará River, which belongs to the greater Amazon River system and is separated from the larger part of the Amazon delta by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marajo">Marajó Island</a>. It is the gateway to the Amazon, with a busy port. The climate is tropical rainforest, with more than 60 mm of rain on average in every month and a wetter season from December to May. The city is called the City of Mango Trees because of the many mango trees in it, and it is named after Santa Maria de Belém in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1615 the Portuguese captain-general Francisco Caldeira Castelo Branco led a military expedition, sent by the Governor General of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>, to check the trading excursions of French, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> and English up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amazon">Amazon</a>. On 12 January 1616 he anchored in Guajará Bay, where the Pará and Guamá rivers meet. Mistaking the bay for the main channel, he built a wooden fort covered with straw, which he called Presépio, meaning <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nativity-scene">nativity scene</a>, now known as Forte do Castelo. The colony it formed was named Feliz Lusitânia and became the beginning of Belém. The fort failed to suppress Dutch and French trading but did ward off colonisation. Belém was the first European colony on the Amazon, and was given city status in 1655.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">Sugar</a> was important in the region until the end of the 17th century, followed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> ranching and, in the 18th century, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a>. Belém then became the main exporting centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amazon">Amazon</a> rubber industry, and its position was further enhanced in 1866 when the Amazon, Tocantins and Tapajós rivers were opened to navigation, although the Port of Manaus upriver reduced its importance somewhat. The rubber era ended after the boom of 1910–12, but Belém remained the main commercial centre of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Belém is the capital and largest city of the state of Pará, in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>. Its colonial quarter keeps tree-filled squares, churches and traditional blue tiles. On the second Sunday in October it holds the Círio of Nazaré, described as the largest religious event in Brazil, a procession that follows a statue of the Virgin and Child and is regarded as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amazon">Amazon</a>; its river pilgrimage honours the water men, who regard the Virgin of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazareth">Nazareth</a> as their patron saint. The Federal University of Pará and the State University of Pará are in the city.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Belém” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Since the return of democracy, freer renderings have been allowed, such as Fafá de <b>Belém</b>'s, criticised at first and now widely accepted, though most singers keep to Nepomuceno's setting.</blockquote><a href="/songs/brazilian-national-anthem">Brazilian National Anthem</a></li><li><blockquote>The Portuguese explorer Francisco Caldeira Castelo Branco founded the city of <b>Belém</b>, today the capital of Pará in Brazil, on the Amazon River delta.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-12">12 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1751 the Marquis of Pombal reorganised the State of Maranhão as the State of Grão-Pará and Maranhão, moving its capital from São Luís to <b>Belém</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-grao-para-and-maranhao">State of Grão-Pará and Maranhão</a></li><li><blockquote>The western part, comprising the Captaincy of Grão-Pará and the Captaincy of São José do Rio Negro, created in 1755 with its seat at Barcelos, became the State of Grão-Pará and Rio Negro, governed from <b>Belém</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-grao-para-and-rio-negro">State of Grão-Pará and Rio Negro</a></li><li><blockquote>After expelling the French of Equinoctial France in 1615 and founding <b>Belém</b> in 1616, Portugal created the State of Maranhão in 1621, separate from the State of Brazil.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-maranhao">State of Maranhão</a></li></ul></section>
